{"id":"q23","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"linda","question":"Bill is 34, intelligent but unimaginative, compulsive, and generally lifeless. In school he was strong in mathematics and weak in social studies and humanities. Is 'Bill is an accountant who plays jazz for a hobby' more probable than 'Bill plays jazz for a hobby'?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The description fits the stereotype of an accountant well.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"Bill's math strength, compulsiveness and lack of imagination match a stereotypical accountant.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The added detail makes the first statement a more coherent, vivid picture, so it feels more plausible.","confidence":0.6,"lean":1,"theta":0.9425,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"The first statement is a conjunction of being an accountant and playing jazz.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Every case where both are true is also a case where Bill plays jazz, so a conjunction cannot be more probable than either part.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Adding detail makes a story more specific and less likely to be true, even if it seems more representative.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The description gives no information bearing on whether Bill plays jazz beyond the base rate.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The conjunction rule P(A and B) ≤ P(B) decides the question regardless of stereotype fit.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The feeling that the first statement is more probable is the representativeness heuristic, not a valid probability judgment.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,